diagnosis_text:
OSPF on router16 is healthy and forwarding. Multiple end-to-end tests from Campus 1 PCs (172.16.x.x) to Campus 2 (10.5.0.x / 10.6.0.x) succeed with 0% loss: for example, pc5→pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc5→pc3 (10.6.0.3) both return three replies. Similar successful probes were observed from pc7, pc8, pc10, pc12, and pc14 to 10.5.0.1 and 10.6.0.3.

On router16, OSPF adjacencies are fully established: “show ip ospf neighbor” reports two neighbors in Full state—one over bond0 to 10.2.2.2 (Router ID 172.16.30.254) and one over eth2 to 172.30.1.2 (Router ID 192.168.10.1). The OSPF RIB and kernel routing table contain active OSPF routes to Campus 2 and backbone prefixes, e.g., 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 via 172.30.1.2, and 172.16.10/20/30 via 10.2.2.2. These routes are installed and in use (proto ospf in “ip route show”).

Localization: router16 interfaces bond0 (10.2.2.1/24) and eth2 (172.30.1.1/24) are up; OSPF area 0 neighbors are stable and recently refreshed. The reported “frr.service inactive (dead)” from systemd is a false positive in this lab; FRR daemons are clearly running (vtysh shows live OSPF neighbors/routes).

Root cause: No current edge fault on router16; the outage report stems from a misleading service status check and/or a transient/non-reproducible issue.

Corrective fix: No repair needed. Update monitoring/runbooks to verify OSPF health via vtysh (e.g., “show ip ospf neighbor/route”) instead of relying on systemd status in this environment. === END ===